WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.128 --> 00:00:08.670
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA:
Hello, and welcome

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:11.790
to "Spanner Internals Part 2."

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:12.990
This is a second talk.

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:16.620
We had a talk yesterday called
"Spanner Internals Part 1."

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:21.180
That was around how sort
of Spanner internally works

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:25.230
and how it's evolved as a
system over time inside Google,

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:27.970
and then we externalize
it into the Cloud.

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:29.910
So today, we're
going to focus on one

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:34.330
of the critical systems built
on top of Spanner internally,

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:36.480
which is Google Cloud Storage.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:40.260
But before that, let's
sort of start out

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:44.580
with, what is Cloud Spanner?

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:49.690
So my name is Deepti Srivastava.

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:52.300
I am the product manager
for Cloud Spanner, which

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:56.960
is our Google Cloud Platform
managed database offering,

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:00.370
which is offered on GCP.

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:06.580
And we talk about it as the
only Enterprise-grade, globally

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:10.160
distributed, and strongly
consistent database service

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.580
which is built
specifically for the Cloud.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:14.420
And what does that really mean?

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:18.520
It means that this
database service combines

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:21.760
the benefit of
relational semantics

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:24.820
with non-relational
traditionally NoSQL

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:26.420
horizontal scale.

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:27.610
So we have schemas.

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:29.320
We have asset transactions.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:30.880
We have SQL.

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:34.780
And we have the ease
of scalability and high

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:37.240
availability, such as
four nines of availability

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:39.460
for our regional
configurations, five nines

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:42.340
of availability for our
multi-region configurations.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:46.630
And all of this is packaged
up in a fully managed database

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:52.370
service that runs on Google's
Cloud infrastructure.

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:56.650
So as I said, the special
thing about Spanner

00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:59.410
that no other database system
in the world currently has

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:03.790
is the ability to do
asset transactions

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:09.490
and have strong consistency
even across WAN and across data

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:11.590
centers and across regions.

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:13.660
No other database
in the world today

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:16.300
can do actual
strong transaction--

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:18.460
a strong consistency
on transactions

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:22.120
when you start to put the
replicas in more than one data

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:23.770
center that are
actually separated

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:28.270
from each other through
a wider network fiber.

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:35.230
And that speciality
allows both our customers

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:39.040
who are using Cloud Spanner to
build interesting applications

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:41.110
on top of it that can
be globally distributed

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:44.210
while having
read-after-write semantics.

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:46.930
It also allows our
internal services

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:50.500
to build some very exciting
and differentiated services

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:52.330
on top of Cloud--

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:54.280
on top of the internal Spanner.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:58.720
So we talk about internal
Spanner and external Spanner,

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:02.660
but what are the real
differences here?

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:06.410
So Spanner, internally
or externally,

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:08.960
is actually the
same infrastructure.

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:11.550
It's the same
binary it's running,

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:14.300
whether it's serving external
stuff or internal stuff.

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:16.550
The only difference
is the access paths.

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:18.520
So externally, we have--

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:21.000
when you're talking
to Cloud Spanner,

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:24.030
the access path is through
the Google Cloud Platform APIs

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:26.720
and through all the security,
and all those kinds of things,

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:30.020
whereas internally, we have
our own internal access paths.

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:32.780
And we keep the data
separated for obvious reasons.

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:34.640
We don't want to mix
external customer

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:36.530
data with internal data.

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:40.430
So obviously, all of
the security and privacy

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:42.710
and compliance aspects apply.

00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:45.350
But in terms of,
are you giving me

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:46.730
a slightly different version?

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:47.410
We're not.

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:50.270
We're giving you the same
Spanner that we have internally

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:52.340
for our critical services.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:54.950
The other differences
are in terminology.

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:59.300
So my colleague will come up
and talk about some of these.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:02.970
But basically, externally,
we have Cloud Spanner nodes,

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:05.720
which we call Spanner
Servers internally.

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:08.270
And externally, we have
instance configurations,

00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:15.080
which are the topology for how a
replication is done-- basically

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:16.430
a replication topology.

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:18.950
And this can be
regional configurations

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:21.140
or multi-region configurations.

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:23.510
For those who are
familiar with Spanner,

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:25.650
Spanner is inherently
replicated.

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:29.480
So we have three replicas
for regional and 5, 7, 9

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:31.250
replicas for
multi-region, depending

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:33.740
on which configuration
we've chosen.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:37.430
And these things are called
sort of Spanner configurations

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:38.330
internally.

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:42.980
So as I said, essentially,
it's the same differentiated

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:45.230
technology that we use
internally and have

00:04:45.230 --> 00:04:47.720
done for seven, eight,
nine years, which

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:50.150
is why we feel so
confident about hosting

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:51.860
our customers on
it in the Cloud,

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:53.780
because it's battle-tested.

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:54.890
It's hardened.

00:04:54.890 --> 00:04:58.070
And most of Google's critical
services at this point

00:04:58.070 --> 00:04:59.600
run on Spanner.

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:05.660
One of those critical services
is Google Cloud Storage, GCS,

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:08.030
which is a very popular service.

00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:09.290
It's built on top of Spanner.

00:05:09.290 --> 00:05:13.130
And because of Spanner's
strong external consistency,

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:18.890
as we call it, GCS
is able to provide

00:05:18.890 --> 00:05:23.390
very differentiated set of
capabilities to its customers.

00:05:23.390 --> 00:05:25.370
So to tell you
about that more, I'd

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:29.172
love to invite Denis, who
is our colleague here.

00:05:29.172 --> 00:05:30.380
Denis, why don't you come up?

00:05:38.168 --> 00:05:39.210
DENIS SERENYI: All right.

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:41.040
Thanks, Deepti.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:43.540
So a little bit about myself.

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:49.680
I'm the tech lead for GCS's
metadata layer for Google Cloud

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:50.880
Storage.

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:57.730
And hopefully, you are already
somewhat familiar with Google

00:05:57.730 --> 00:06:00.400
Cloud Storage and/or
maybe have even

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:03.010
seen one of our other talks.

00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:06.490
But I'm going to
talk a little bit

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:10.030
about-- give you an overview
of what GCS is all about,

00:06:10.030 --> 00:06:16.630
and then go into depth in how
GCS takes advantage of Spanner.

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:21.750
So GCS is object
storage made simple.

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:26.650
It's a central repository for
all your organization's data,

00:06:26.650 --> 00:06:31.830
putting it under a single
consistent unified API.

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:36.010
And so GCS is able to store
a wide variety of data,

00:06:36.010 --> 00:06:39.030
whether it be content-serving
data, whether it

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:43.830
be analytics data that requires
high bandwidth, or even

00:06:43.830 --> 00:06:48.060
warm/cold storage,
or archival tiers.

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:49.740
It's highly reliable.

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:53.970
GCS provides up to four
nines of availability

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:55.455
and multi-regional storage.

00:06:57.990 --> 00:07:00.110
It's extremely cost-effective.

00:07:00.110 --> 00:07:04.560
GCS can store hot data, but
it also stores cold data,

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:10.930
offering a price per gigabyte
as low as $0.04 a gigabyte.

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:12.930
Or sorry, $0.004 a gigabyte.

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:16.510
And we just introduced
our archival storage tier,

00:07:16.510 --> 00:07:21.710
which is as low as
$0.12 per gigabyte.

00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:23.790
GCS is secure.

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:26.330
It's a great way of
keeping your data secure.

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:29.900
Its data is encrypted
at rest and in transit

00:07:29.900 --> 00:07:33.890
when stored in GCS and were
integrated with Cloud storage

00:07:33.890 --> 00:07:36.410
solutions like IAM and KMS.

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:42.980
GCS offers three
different location types.

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:45.420
There is what's called
regional storage.

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:47.780
And that's where your
data is stored redundantly

00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:51.800
within one of our Cloud
regions across availability

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:53.990
zones within that region.

00:07:53.990 --> 00:07:58.970
And that's really good
for analytics data.

00:07:58.970 --> 00:08:01.100
This regional storage
is a great way

00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:05.240
of ensuring that your data is
co-located with your compute

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:09.680
so you can get really high
bandwidth access to your data

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:12.230
within that region.

00:08:12.230 --> 00:08:14.750
Our second location
type is what's

00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:16.470
called multi-regional storage.

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:21.530
And that's where your data
is spread redundantly across

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:26.780
multiple regions
within a multi-region--

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:31.160
a multi-region being
US, Asia, or EU.

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:34.190
And this is really great
for content serving.

00:08:34.190 --> 00:08:36.679
We have over 100
points of presence

00:08:36.679 --> 00:08:39.200
where this data can
be stored, which

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:42.380
means that if you're
doing content serving,

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:47.390
oftentimes the data is located
very close to where it's being

00:08:47.390 --> 00:08:50.870
requested, and so we
can minimize latency,

00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:53.870
minimize bandwidth by
distributing it widely

00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:56.690
across a multi-region.

00:08:56.690 --> 00:08:59.750
And then the third location
type is dual-regional.

00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:01.910
This is something that
we've introduced recently

00:09:01.910 --> 00:09:05.570
that kind of combines
the best of both worlds.

00:09:05.570 --> 00:09:09.890
Dual-regional is a
great way of ensuring

00:09:09.890 --> 00:09:13.430
that your data is
located in two specific--

00:09:13.430 --> 00:09:15.960
in a specific pair of regions.

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:19.460
And this way, you
can ensure that you

00:09:19.460 --> 00:09:22.580
have really high bandwidth
access to that storage

00:09:22.580 --> 00:09:24.560
within those two regions.

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:29.190
Say, if you want to use
that data for analytics,

00:09:29.190 --> 00:09:32.270
but you can get higher
availability with dual-regional

00:09:32.270 --> 00:09:34.180
than you can get with regional.

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:38.330
So if you need to have an
analytics application where

00:09:38.330 --> 00:09:41.510
you need to potentially
failover between regions,

00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:44.970
dual-regional is a great
way of providing that.

00:09:44.970 --> 00:09:47.360
And Twitter is an
example of a user

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:49.400
that's using our
dual-regional storage right

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:50.680
now for analytics.

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:52.820
Vimeo is an example
of a user that's

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:55.850
using our multi-regional
storage for content serving.

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:04.400
So GCS provides a
lot of key features.

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:08.000
This is an example of four
key differentiating features

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:09.500
of GCS.

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:12.620
And what's important to
keep in mind is that--

00:10:12.620 --> 00:10:14.930
as I'm going to go
into in more detail--

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:19.460
GCS uses Spanner heavily under
the hood in its implementation,

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:24.080
and all of these features are
in some way enabled by the fact

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:26.720
that GCS is using Spanner.

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:28.640
So let's talk about
the first one--

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:30.740
strongly consistent listings.

00:10:30.740 --> 00:10:34.850
What this means is that when
you do an object listing in GCS,

00:10:34.850 --> 00:10:38.450
you're guaranteed to see
exactly what's there, not

00:10:38.450 --> 00:10:40.070
some partial view.

00:10:40.070 --> 00:10:45.170
And this makes your applications
on top of storage much simpler

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:51.200
because you can rely on your
storage layer being consistent.

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:55.470
GCS offers geo-redundancy
within the system.

00:10:55.470 --> 00:11:01.220
So as opposed to systems where
you have to sort of cobble

00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:06.680
together geo-redundancy at
layers above the storage system

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:12.710
by adding replication and
cross-regional replication,

00:11:12.710 --> 00:11:15.380
GCS offers that as part--

00:11:15.380 --> 00:11:17.300
just in one package.

00:11:17.300 --> 00:11:19.880
And we of course
leverage the fact

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:22.910
that Spanner's metadata
is geo-redundant

00:11:22.910 --> 00:11:24.930
in order to provide this.

00:11:24.930 --> 00:11:27.320
GCS is highly scalable.

00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:30.800
So much like Spanner
is used internally

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:33.920
very heavily by
Google, GCS is also

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:38.040
used really heavily by
many systems across Google.

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:40.220
And so we are--

00:11:40.220 --> 00:11:43.130
we've had to grow
to Google scale just

00:11:43.130 --> 00:11:47.070
to support Google's
internal applications.

00:11:47.070 --> 00:11:51.070
And so we expose that to
our Cloud users as well.

00:11:51.070 --> 00:11:53.780
GCS is scalable to exabytes.

00:11:53.780 --> 00:11:58.970
And the fact that we're using
Spanner as our metadata storage

00:11:58.970 --> 00:12:02.580
within GCS really
facilitates this scalability.

00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:05.600
So because we have a highly
scalable metadata layer,

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:10.970
it allows our storage system
to scale to these levels.

00:12:10.970 --> 00:12:14.930
And GCS provides a
single unified experience

00:12:14.930 --> 00:12:18.330
for all the variety
of data that you have.

00:12:18.330 --> 00:12:22.400
So regardless of what
storage classes you're using

00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:27.560
or what location types, there
is a single unified API.

00:12:27.560 --> 00:12:31.100
And that is also
facilitated by Spanner.

00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:34.610
Because we're using Spanner
as our centralized metadata

00:12:34.610 --> 00:12:37.940
repository, it
allows us to expose--

00:12:37.940 --> 00:12:40.400
for all of the
data that's in GCS,

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:44.150
it allows us to expose the
single unified metadata

00:12:44.150 --> 00:12:45.710
layer on top--

00:12:45.710 --> 00:12:49.770
API on top of that metadata.

00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:51.600
So now let's dive
a little bit more

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:57.050
deeply into how GCS uses
Spanner under the hood.

00:12:57.050 --> 00:13:01.730
So you're probably familiar
with the GCS core storage

00:13:01.730 --> 00:13:02.720
abstractions.

00:13:02.720 --> 00:13:06.020
And these are
buckets and objects.

00:13:06.020 --> 00:13:11.750
Simply, an object is a container
for a collection of data,

00:13:11.750 --> 00:13:14.950
like a file in a file system.

00:13:14.950 --> 00:13:17.810
And objects have
a particular name.

00:13:17.810 --> 00:13:21.890
There's also buckets, which
are collections of objects.

00:13:21.890 --> 00:13:26.120
And both of these things have
associated metadata in GCS.

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:29.900
So there is bucket metadata
and object metadata.

00:13:29.900 --> 00:13:37.470
And that metadata is stored
in databases in Spanner.

00:13:37.470 --> 00:13:40.460
GCS is using, much
like Deepti alluded to,

00:13:40.460 --> 00:13:43.580
GCS is using Spanner
much like any other user

00:13:43.580 --> 00:13:45.320
is using Spanner.

00:13:45.320 --> 00:13:48.840
It's the same system.

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:52.005
And so we're basically
using Spanner just as

00:13:52.005 --> 00:13:52.880
an ordinary customer.

00:13:55.450 --> 00:13:58.950
So I would say that
Spanner provides

00:13:58.950 --> 00:14:02.370
four key differentiating
features that

00:14:02.370 --> 00:14:04.710
are critical for
GCS's implementation

00:14:04.710 --> 00:14:07.520
and critical for GCS's use case.

00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:10.740
And the first one is
strong consistency.

00:14:10.740 --> 00:14:14.070
And so what that means for
us in our implementation

00:14:14.070 --> 00:14:18.900
is that when we do mutations
into our Spanner databases,

00:14:18.900 --> 00:14:22.800
those updates are
immediately visible to all

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:25.290
of GCS's front end systems.

00:14:25.290 --> 00:14:29.310
And that allows us to
provide our own user

00:14:29.310 --> 00:14:33.630
visible consistency
semantics much more simply.

00:14:33.630 --> 00:14:35.880
It's certainly possible
for storage systems

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:38.280
to offer the kind of
strong consistency

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:42.060
that we offer without a
strongly consistent database,

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:45.720
but that would add
tons of complexity.

00:14:45.720 --> 00:14:50.520
Storage systems can do that
with coherent caching layers

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:54.030
or fancy request
routing, or having master

00:14:54.030 --> 00:14:55.830
election in the storage system.

00:14:55.830 --> 00:14:58.170
But we kind of don't
have to do any of that.

00:14:58.170 --> 00:14:59.430
We don't have to deal with it.

00:14:59.430 --> 00:15:01.830
We just put our
metadata in Spanner

00:15:01.830 --> 00:15:04.530
and serve that metadata
out of Spanner,

00:15:04.530 --> 00:15:06.930
and it just kind of takes
care of the consistency

00:15:06.930 --> 00:15:09.300
issues for us.

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:12.960
Spanner has a really
simple programming model.

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:16.290
It supports single-row
transactions,

00:15:16.290 --> 00:15:18.690
and we do mostly
single-row transactions.

00:15:18.690 --> 00:15:22.080
But we also use
multi-row transactions,

00:15:22.080 --> 00:15:24.090
multi-table
transactions, and even

00:15:24.090 --> 00:15:26.370
multi-database transactions.

00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:30.990
And this allows our system
to be much simpler in how

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:32.780
it interacts with metadata.

00:15:32.780 --> 00:15:36.570
GCS is made up of many
independent components

00:15:36.570 --> 00:15:37.380
under the hood.

00:15:37.380 --> 00:15:41.430
And a lot of those components
are interacting actually

00:15:41.430 --> 00:15:43.350
directly with metadata.

00:15:43.350 --> 00:15:46.830
And so the fact that
all of these systems

00:15:46.830 --> 00:15:50.760
can interact transactionally
in a variety of different ways

00:15:50.760 --> 00:15:55.620
with our metadata databases,
it makes all of those systems

00:15:55.620 --> 00:15:59.880
pretty simple, and it gives
us a lot of confidence

00:15:59.880 --> 00:16:04.660
that our metadata is
correct and consistent.

00:16:04.660 --> 00:16:07.560
The fact that Spanner supports
really high availability

00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:09.190
is critical for our use case.

00:16:09.190 --> 00:16:13.770
So we use geo-redundant
Spanner deployments.

00:16:13.770 --> 00:16:17.430
And the fact that you only
need a quorum of zones

00:16:17.430 --> 00:16:20.070
available at any one time
in order to be serving

00:16:20.070 --> 00:16:23.340
is something that we rely on.

00:16:23.340 --> 00:16:25.620
GCS is also pretty big.

00:16:25.620 --> 00:16:29.130
So the fact that Spanner
has really excellent

00:16:29.130 --> 00:16:34.170
horizontal scaling is very
important for our use case.

00:16:34.170 --> 00:16:37.230
Simply put, when one
of our locations--

00:16:37.230 --> 00:16:39.300
when the metadata in
one of those locations

00:16:39.300 --> 00:16:42.250
grows to the point where
we need more resources,

00:16:42.250 --> 00:16:45.660
we just kind of scale up
our Spanner instances.

00:16:45.660 --> 00:16:48.540
And that's a pretty simple
administrative action.

00:16:48.540 --> 00:16:51.570
And Spanner scalability
just kind of takes

00:16:51.570 --> 00:16:54.870
care of the fact that
location needs to grow.

00:16:54.870 --> 00:16:57.060
I'm sure that there
are scalability limits.

00:16:57.060 --> 00:16:59.260
I mean, every system
does have them.

00:16:59.260 --> 00:17:01.710
But we just haven't
hit those limits.

00:17:01.710 --> 00:17:06.060
And GCS is already really big.

00:17:06.060 --> 00:17:10.380
So just to give you an idea,
some idea of what kind of scale

00:17:10.380 --> 00:17:14.970
we're talking about when we're
talking about how big GCS is,

00:17:14.970 --> 00:17:18.599
this is an example of
one of our multi-regions

00:17:18.599 --> 00:17:21.839
and some high-level data
points for how big we've

00:17:21.839 --> 00:17:24.450
been able to scale
our Spanner databases

00:17:24.450 --> 00:17:26.260
for this multi-region.

00:17:26.260 --> 00:17:29.880
So we're talking about
trillions of database rows,

00:17:29.880 --> 00:17:32.670
millions of queries
per second, millions

00:17:32.670 --> 00:17:37.230
of mutations per second,
petabytes of logical data

00:17:37.230 --> 00:17:40.080
stored in these databases.

00:17:40.080 --> 00:17:42.520
We're using thousands
of Spanner servers,

00:17:42.520 --> 00:17:44.720
even for this one multi-region.

00:17:44.720 --> 00:17:50.050
And there is over 1,000
miles between Spanner zones.

00:17:50.050 --> 00:17:54.210
So the fact that we have
been able to scale up

00:17:54.210 --> 00:17:59.880
our databases and a Spanner
instance to this scale,

00:17:59.880 --> 00:18:03.030
and we haven't sacrificed
our strong consistency

00:18:03.030 --> 00:18:06.120
or high availability is
really pretty unique.

00:18:06.120 --> 00:18:11.180
It's pretty cool that we've been
able to take advantage of this.

00:18:11.180 --> 00:18:13.420
So let's return to
the earlier slide

00:18:13.420 --> 00:18:16.750
where we were talking
about GCS's location types

00:18:16.750 --> 00:18:19.750
and talk about exactly what that
means in terms of our Spanner

00:18:19.750 --> 00:18:21.100
deployments.

00:18:21.100 --> 00:18:25.660
For regional storage, we're
using a Spanner instance that

00:18:25.660 --> 00:18:30.260
is entirely within a region.

00:18:30.260 --> 00:18:33.550
And so just to keep in mind, we
use separate Spanner instances

00:18:33.550 --> 00:18:36.340
for all GCS locations.

00:18:36.340 --> 00:18:39.790
And so for regional, we
use a Spanner instance

00:18:39.790 --> 00:18:42.700
that's within the
region and zones

00:18:42.700 --> 00:18:44.770
that are within
different availability

00:18:44.770 --> 00:18:46.690
zones of that region.

00:18:46.690 --> 00:18:50.200
For multi-regional, we
use a Spanner instance--

00:18:50.200 --> 00:18:51.880
I'm calling them
Alex in the slide.

00:18:51.880 --> 00:18:53.380
That's what we call
them internally.

00:18:53.380 --> 00:18:55.930
It's just a different name--

00:18:55.930 --> 00:18:58.780
with zones that are
spread across the regions

00:18:58.780 --> 00:19:02.050
within the multi-region.

00:19:02.050 --> 00:19:05.770
And for dual-regional,
we use a Spanner instance

00:19:05.770 --> 00:19:10.750
that has zones within the two
regions of the multi-region

00:19:10.750 --> 00:19:12.090
plus a third region.

00:19:15.010 --> 00:19:17.770
So now let's deep
dive into really

00:19:17.770 --> 00:19:21.610
exactly how Spanner's
strong consistency is so

00:19:21.610 --> 00:19:26.160
important for GCS's use case.

00:19:26.160 --> 00:19:28.460
So I've already talked
about this a little bit,

00:19:28.460 --> 00:19:31.040
but I want to go
into depth into how

00:19:31.040 --> 00:19:35.720
important strong consistency
is for GCS users.

00:19:35.720 --> 00:19:39.110
Simply put, it's much
simpler to build applications

00:19:39.110 --> 00:19:43.610
on top of storage when the
storage system is consistent,

00:19:43.610 --> 00:19:46.990
when you're getting a
complete view of what's

00:19:46.990 --> 00:19:49.790
in your storage system
at any one point in time.

00:19:52.910 --> 00:19:56.030
So as a specific
example, GCS offers

00:19:56.030 --> 00:19:58.580
read-after-write
consistency, which

00:19:58.580 --> 00:20:02.570
means when you write
an object into GCS

00:20:02.570 --> 00:20:04.550
and then later try
to read it back,

00:20:04.550 --> 00:20:06.620
even if it's one of
our multi-regions

00:20:06.620 --> 00:20:10.430
and we're doing sort of
cross-region redundancies

00:20:10.430 --> 00:20:14.600
with that data, we have-- we
provide a strong guarantee

00:20:14.600 --> 00:20:18.230
that you read exactly
what you wrote earlier.

00:20:18.230 --> 00:20:20.360
And it sounds kind
of obvious, like I

00:20:20.360 --> 00:20:23.420
mean, I guess, really every
storage system should work

00:20:23.420 --> 00:20:26.030
this way, but there's a lot
of storage systems out there

00:20:26.030 --> 00:20:28.250
that don't actually
provide this guarantee,

00:20:28.250 --> 00:20:30.330
that are only
eventually consistent,

00:20:30.330 --> 00:20:33.920
especially when you're
talking about geo-redundancy.

00:20:33.920 --> 00:20:37.730
And this makes
applications a lot simpler.

00:20:37.730 --> 00:20:40.010
If you don't have
this guarantee,

00:20:40.010 --> 00:20:42.740
you might have to
introduce retry loops

00:20:42.740 --> 00:20:44.388
into your application.

00:20:44.388 --> 00:20:44.930
I don't know.

00:20:44.930 --> 00:20:47.630
If the object isn't there
and you try and read it

00:20:47.630 --> 00:20:51.390
and it's not, then you might
have to just kind of like

00:20:51.390 --> 00:20:52.655
wait and retry later.

00:20:55.430 --> 00:20:58.700
Another reason why strong
consistency is so important

00:20:58.700 --> 00:21:03.500
is that GCS is being used
as an organization's what's

00:21:03.500 --> 00:21:06.050
called a data lake, which
means that there are

00:21:06.050 --> 00:21:10.880
many applications that
are working together

00:21:10.880 --> 00:21:16.310
on top of storage that's
centrally located within GCS.

00:21:16.310 --> 00:21:20.420
And if all of those applications
don't have a guarantee

00:21:20.420 --> 00:21:23.630
that they're seeing exactly
what's in that shared storage

00:21:23.630 --> 00:21:28.850
system at a point in time, then
it also adds more complexity

00:21:28.850 --> 00:21:30.110
to those applications.

00:21:30.110 --> 00:21:31.790
They might have to
deal with the fact

00:21:31.790 --> 00:21:36.470
that there is only a partial
view that they're seeing.

00:21:36.470 --> 00:21:40.130
A specific example of this might
be like an analytics pipeline,

00:21:40.130 --> 00:21:46.640
where there is one stage
of a Hadoop pipeline

00:21:46.640 --> 00:21:48.800
that is producing a data set.

00:21:48.800 --> 00:21:51.140
And that data set
is later consumed

00:21:51.140 --> 00:21:53.750
by some other stage
of the pipeline, which

00:21:53.750 --> 00:21:56.880
is doing additional
transformations on that data.

00:21:56.880 --> 00:22:00.320
If that later stage doesn't
have a strong guarantee

00:22:00.320 --> 00:22:04.730
that it sees everything that was
produced by that first stage,

00:22:04.730 --> 00:22:06.993
it's going to be some extra--

00:22:06.993 --> 00:22:09.410
you're going to have to deal
with that in your application

00:22:09.410 --> 00:22:10.520
somehow.

00:22:10.520 --> 00:22:13.390
And a lot of times it's
just hard to deal with that.

00:22:16.050 --> 00:22:20.120
So in addition to strong
consistency provided by Spanner

00:22:20.120 --> 00:22:23.090
being really beneficial
for our users,

00:22:23.090 --> 00:22:27.650
GCS also takes advantage of
Spanner's strong consistency

00:22:27.650 --> 00:22:30.710
within the
implementation of GCS.

00:22:30.710 --> 00:22:33.260
So how many people
in the audience

00:22:33.260 --> 00:22:35.600
are familiar enough
with GCS to know

00:22:35.600 --> 00:22:38.660
what a compose operation is?

00:22:38.660 --> 00:22:39.160
OK.

00:22:39.160 --> 00:22:41.200
Not too many people.

00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:46.960
So compose is an operation
that's provided by GCS.

00:22:46.960 --> 00:22:52.060
And it's a way to take
several-source objects

00:22:52.060 --> 00:22:57.430
and stitch them together into
a single-destination object.

00:22:57.430 --> 00:23:01.030
This is typically used, say,
for uploading large objects

00:23:01.030 --> 00:23:01.750
into the system.

00:23:01.750 --> 00:23:04.370
You can upload the
object in pieces,

00:23:04.370 --> 00:23:08.410
and then after the fact,
stitch all the pieces together

00:23:08.410 --> 00:23:10.690
into a single object.

00:23:10.690 --> 00:23:14.470
Now, compose clearly
is going to involve

00:23:14.470 --> 00:23:16.900
some kind of a
multi-row transaction

00:23:16.900 --> 00:23:21.160
here, because it's going to be
operating on multiple objects

00:23:21.160 --> 00:23:23.020
and doing that atomically.

00:23:23.020 --> 00:23:27.460
But compose also offers what's
called preconditions, which

00:23:27.460 --> 00:23:30.520
means that for each of the
source objects that you

00:23:30.520 --> 00:23:35.950
specify, you can specify that
the operation should only

00:23:35.950 --> 00:23:38.810
execute if certain
preconditions are met.

00:23:38.810 --> 00:23:41.410
For instance, the
objects have to match

00:23:41.410 --> 00:23:47.270
a particular generation in order
for the operation to complete.

00:23:47.270 --> 00:23:51.970
So clearly, here is a case
where strong consistency is

00:23:51.970 --> 00:23:53.720
going to be really important.

00:23:53.720 --> 00:23:55.840
We're going to want to
read the metadata for all

00:23:55.840 --> 00:23:59.560
of these objects out
of Spanner and evaluate

00:23:59.560 --> 00:24:03.100
these preconditions, and then
if the preconditions evaluate,

00:24:03.100 --> 00:24:04.120
commit a mutation.

00:24:04.120 --> 00:24:07.090
If we don't have strong
consistency guarantees

00:24:07.090 --> 00:24:09.130
from our database,
it's kind of a lot

00:24:09.130 --> 00:24:11.530
harder to implement
that operation.

00:24:14.090 --> 00:24:16.310
So here's another
example of how we're

00:24:16.310 --> 00:24:19.100
taking advantage of
Spanner's strong consistency.

00:24:19.100 --> 00:24:24.710
So GCS has an FSCK, a
Filesystem Consistency Checker.

00:24:24.710 --> 00:24:29.900
This is something that
most storage systems have.

00:24:29.900 --> 00:24:33.590
It's a process where
in the background,

00:24:33.590 --> 00:24:36.740
you have something-- the FSCK
checker is something that's

00:24:36.740 --> 00:24:39.200
running in the
background and validating

00:24:39.200 --> 00:24:42.080
that all of the metadata
in the storage system

00:24:42.080 --> 00:24:46.010
is correct, that it's
free of inconsistencies.

00:24:46.010 --> 00:24:49.010
And a lot of times,
FSCK checkers

00:24:49.010 --> 00:24:51.950
want to do what's
called cross-checking.

00:24:51.950 --> 00:24:55.700
So validate that the
contents of one database

00:24:55.700 --> 00:25:00.950
is consistent with the
value in another database.

00:25:00.950 --> 00:25:05.480
And in order to make these kinds
of checks simple within GCS,

00:25:05.480 --> 00:25:07.400
we take advantage
of the fact that you

00:25:07.400 --> 00:25:13.680
can take a consistent point in
time snapshot across databases.

00:25:13.680 --> 00:25:17.540
And we have a strong guarantee
from Spanner that everything

00:25:17.540 --> 00:25:19.610
that's in that database, even--

00:25:19.610 --> 00:25:21.380
I'm sorry-- in that
snapshot, even if it's

00:25:21.380 --> 00:25:24.560
multiple databases,
is exactly what was

00:25:24.560 --> 00:25:27.720
in those databases at
that point in time.

00:25:27.720 --> 00:25:31.670
So this would make our FSCK
cross-checking much simpler.

00:25:31.670 --> 00:25:35.490
In this example-- it's
kind of a simple example,

00:25:35.490 --> 00:25:39.230
but this is an actual check
that we have in our FSCK, which

00:25:39.230 --> 00:25:43.040
is that if there is a table
of all of your objects

00:25:43.040 --> 00:25:46.340
in a given bucket and
then a separate table

00:25:46.340 --> 00:25:51.920
in a separate database that
shows the state of the bucket,

00:25:51.920 --> 00:25:54.320
whether it's deleted
or not, pretty clearly,

00:25:54.320 --> 00:25:55.850
if there's objects
in the bucket,

00:25:55.850 --> 00:25:57.540
then the bucket
shouldn't be deleted.

00:25:57.540 --> 00:26:04.220
So we want to validate that the
contents of this one database

00:26:04.220 --> 00:26:08.270
as refers to this one bucket is
consistent with the information

00:26:08.270 --> 00:26:09.620
about objects in the bucket.

00:26:12.210 --> 00:26:17.210
So here's-- my third example
is that we're using secondary

00:26:17.210 --> 00:26:20.090
indexes within GCS.

00:26:20.090 --> 00:26:23.660
And the way that we take
advantage of secondary indexes

00:26:23.660 --> 00:26:28.280
is to optimize the performance
of our background scanners.

00:26:28.280 --> 00:26:30.830
So storage systems.

00:26:30.830 --> 00:26:32.810
It's fairly common
for storage systems

00:26:32.810 --> 00:26:35.000
to have background scanners.

00:26:35.000 --> 00:26:37.280
And what these
things typically do

00:26:37.280 --> 00:26:39.920
is things like
maintenance operations.

00:26:39.920 --> 00:26:43.070
So you might have
a scanner that's

00:26:43.070 --> 00:26:45.080
running over all
of your metadata

00:26:45.080 --> 00:26:50.600
periodically and doing
maintenance on the data that's

00:26:50.600 --> 00:26:53.660
represented by that metadata.

00:26:53.660 --> 00:26:57.680
Another way that background
scanners are used

00:26:57.680 --> 00:26:59.850
is deferred operations.

00:26:59.850 --> 00:27:04.520
So if, say, you do a delete
on some entity in your storage

00:27:04.520 --> 00:27:08.450
system, typically, that delete
is [? act ?] very quickly.

00:27:08.450 --> 00:27:11.330
And it kicks off some
asynchronous work

00:27:11.330 --> 00:27:13.400
to sort of like
actually clean up

00:27:13.400 --> 00:27:17.000
the storage that's
represented by that

00:27:17.000 --> 00:27:18.620
object that you're deleting.

00:27:18.620 --> 00:27:20.840
And so that's sort of a
deferred action that's often

00:27:20.840 --> 00:27:23.210
driven by a background scanner.

00:27:23.210 --> 00:27:26.840
So by using secondary
indexes, and

00:27:26.840 --> 00:27:29.630
in particular sparse
secondary indexes,

00:27:29.630 --> 00:27:32.750
we're able to build
background scanners that

00:27:32.750 --> 00:27:38.060
are much quicker at iterating
over our metadata, because only

00:27:38.060 --> 00:27:41.420
the things that actually need
attention from the background

00:27:41.420 --> 00:27:45.860
scanner are represented in
the table that we're scanning.

00:27:45.860 --> 00:27:49.550
So in this example, we
have an object table.

00:27:49.550 --> 00:27:52.520
And there is a column
in that object table

00:27:52.520 --> 00:27:56.000
that represents the time
at which an update was

00:27:56.000 --> 00:28:00.080
made to each object.

00:28:00.080 --> 00:28:03.290
And it's only when
these objects have

00:28:03.290 --> 00:28:07.250
been updated that the
background scanner actually

00:28:07.250 --> 00:28:09.290
needs to pay attention
to those objects.

00:28:09.290 --> 00:28:11.990
And so we use that
update time as the key

00:28:11.990 --> 00:28:13.850
in a secondary index.

00:28:13.850 --> 00:28:18.980
And then so that secondary index
is much smaller than the object

00:28:18.980 --> 00:28:19.790
table.

00:28:19.790 --> 00:28:21.470
And so the background
scanner is running

00:28:21.470 --> 00:28:23.780
over that secondary index.

00:28:23.780 --> 00:28:26.960
And it's much simpler, actually,
to implement that background

00:28:26.960 --> 00:28:29.510
scanner because the
secondary index is

00:28:29.510 --> 00:28:35.670
consistent with the
object table itself.

00:28:35.670 --> 00:28:38.510
So the last thing that
I wanted to talk about

00:28:38.510 --> 00:28:43.490
is just some of the
challenges that we've

00:28:43.490 --> 00:28:47.290
faced when building our system.

00:28:47.290 --> 00:28:50.360
And one of the challenges
that we do face

00:28:50.360 --> 00:28:53.420
is something called hotspotting.

00:28:53.420 --> 00:28:56.540
So what is hotspotting?

00:28:56.540 --> 00:29:03.680
The thing is that every single
system has scaling limits that

00:29:03.680 --> 00:29:05.930
apply to individual keys.

00:29:05.930 --> 00:29:11.480
So hotspotting can occur if
you have an uneven distribution

00:29:11.480 --> 00:29:14.150
of load within your system.

00:29:14.150 --> 00:29:20.240
Say, for instance, you
have a table of videos.

00:29:20.240 --> 00:29:24.500
And one of those videos
happens to be "Gangnam Style."

00:29:24.500 --> 00:29:27.260
And that's represented
by one row in your table.

00:29:27.260 --> 00:29:31.310
Clearly, that row is going
to be really popular.

00:29:31.310 --> 00:29:34.340
And you're going to have to deal
with some kind of a hotspotting

00:29:34.340 --> 00:29:35.940
problem on that row.

00:29:35.940 --> 00:29:39.890
Now, systems can deal
with this by caching

00:29:39.890 --> 00:29:44.340
whatever is in that metadata
and distributing it more widely.

00:29:44.340 --> 00:29:48.950
We choose to not sacrifice our
strong consistency guarantees

00:29:48.950 --> 00:29:53.210
while attempting to
deal with this problem.

00:29:53.210 --> 00:29:54.980
But we have developed
some techniques

00:29:54.980 --> 00:29:57.980
to deal with it that
I'm going to go into.

00:29:57.980 --> 00:30:02.690
So just for some
background, hotspotting--

00:30:02.690 --> 00:30:05.720
strong reads in Spanner
require going to the leader

00:30:05.720 --> 00:30:07.760
to get the latest timestamp.

00:30:07.760 --> 00:30:11.960
So that is going to
hotspot particular servers.

00:30:11.960 --> 00:30:16.730
And if you aren't careful
about this in your application

00:30:16.730 --> 00:30:20.060
design, it is something that
can limit overall application

00:30:20.060 --> 00:30:22.940
performance.

00:30:22.940 --> 00:30:24.580
So there are two
techniques that we

00:30:24.580 --> 00:30:29.200
employ to minimize
the issues that

00:30:29.200 --> 00:30:31.120
are created by hotspotting.

00:30:31.120 --> 00:30:35.080
And the first one is
through schema design.

00:30:35.080 --> 00:30:38.530
So if the hotspotting
problem that you're facing

00:30:38.530 --> 00:30:43.780
is that there are
small ranges of keys

00:30:43.780 --> 00:30:47.740
that can get much hotter
than other ranges,

00:30:47.740 --> 00:30:50.080
say, for instance,
in this example,

00:30:50.080 --> 00:30:54.180
a collection of objects that
are all very similarly named.

00:30:54.180 --> 00:30:58.060
So they share a
given key prefix.

00:30:58.060 --> 00:31:00.970
You can address any
potential hotspotting

00:31:00.970 --> 00:31:04.300
that might occur on that
range of your key space

00:31:04.300 --> 00:31:08.710
by just spreading it out
more widely in your key space

00:31:08.710 --> 00:31:11.720
by applying what we
call a sharding factor.

00:31:11.720 --> 00:31:15.700
So you can basically
compute a hash, maybe,

00:31:15.700 --> 00:31:19.210
over the contents
of your primary key

00:31:19.210 --> 00:31:23.410
and prefix the actual
primary key with that hash.

00:31:23.410 --> 00:31:27.100
It's going to have the
effect of spreading out

00:31:27.100 --> 00:31:30.790
otherwise co-located
keys much more widely.

00:31:30.790 --> 00:31:34.180
And that spreading
is going to cause

00:31:34.180 --> 00:31:37.960
these keys to tend to fall into
different Spanner splits, which

00:31:37.960 --> 00:31:40.750
will improve your
ability to scale.

00:31:40.750 --> 00:31:44.710
But keep in mind that
this kind of spreading

00:31:44.710 --> 00:31:48.110
can come with a cost,
depending on your workload.

00:31:48.110 --> 00:31:52.600
So if an important
workload for your use case

00:31:52.600 --> 00:31:58.250
is sequential scanning
over your primary key,

00:31:58.250 --> 00:32:01.000
then this kind of spreading can
make that kind of sequential

00:32:01.000 --> 00:32:04.090
scanning a little bit more
expensive, because instead

00:32:04.090 --> 00:32:07.420
of scanning just one small
range of your key space,

00:32:07.420 --> 00:32:12.220
you now have to scan several
ranges of your key space,

00:32:12.220 --> 00:32:14.890
depending on how you've
broken up your primary key.

00:32:17.740 --> 00:32:20.680
So the other way
that we've mitigated

00:32:20.680 --> 00:32:23.020
the issues caused
by hotspotting is

00:32:23.020 --> 00:32:25.660
by using bounded
staleness reads.

00:32:25.660 --> 00:32:29.820
Now, you might be a little
bit sort of wondering, OK,

00:32:29.820 --> 00:32:31.570
I've been talking about
strong consistency

00:32:31.570 --> 00:32:32.890
through this whole talk.

00:32:32.890 --> 00:32:35.530
Now we're talking about
bounded staleness.

00:32:35.530 --> 00:32:38.980
But you can actually-- the nice
thing about Spanner is that you

00:32:38.980 --> 00:32:42.850
can pick and choose when you
can introduce bounded staleness

00:32:42.850 --> 00:32:45.820
into your application
and where--

00:32:45.820 --> 00:32:48.640
and use it only where
it won't jeopardize

00:32:48.640 --> 00:32:50.530
the kinds of high
level semantics

00:32:50.530 --> 00:32:53.330
that you want to provide
in your application.

00:32:53.330 --> 00:32:55.900
So we don't use bounded
staleness everywhere.

00:32:55.900 --> 00:33:00.370
We use it where it won't
jeopardize the semantics

00:33:00.370 --> 00:33:03.980
that we want to
provide to our user.

00:33:03.980 --> 00:33:06.550
And keep in mind that
bounded staleness reads

00:33:06.550 --> 00:33:09.340
are actually much cheaper
than strong reads.

00:33:09.340 --> 00:33:13.840
And that's because, typically,
bounded staleness reads

00:33:13.840 --> 00:33:16.450
don't have to go to the leader.

00:33:16.450 --> 00:33:21.460
Oftentimes, all of the replicas
in your Spanner database

00:33:21.460 --> 00:33:24.400
are almost entirely up-to-date.

00:33:24.400 --> 00:33:27.010
It's only just that
last small window

00:33:27.010 --> 00:33:30.760
of transactions that
haven't yet been propagated

00:33:30.760 --> 00:33:32.570
to all of the replicas.

00:33:32.570 --> 00:33:36.580
And so even if you just
have a small staleness bound

00:33:36.580 --> 00:33:40.308
in your query, it
will almost always not

00:33:40.308 --> 00:33:41.350
have to go to the leader.

00:33:41.350 --> 00:33:44.200
It can go to any replica.

00:33:44.200 --> 00:33:47.185
And what's nice
about Spanner's API,

00:33:47.185 --> 00:33:49.900
is that you can easily
specify how much staleness

00:33:49.900 --> 00:33:51.460
you're willing to tolerate.

00:33:51.460 --> 00:33:54.640
And Spanner will provide
a strong guarantee

00:33:54.640 --> 00:33:57.280
that that staleness
bound that you provide

00:33:57.280 --> 00:34:00.370
is adhered to strictly.

00:34:00.370 --> 00:34:02.920
So here's an example
where we're using

00:34:02.920 --> 00:34:09.800
bounded staleness reads in GCS
as a performance optimization.

00:34:09.800 --> 00:34:13.060
And we're not jeopardizing our
strong consistency guarantees

00:34:13.060 --> 00:34:15.250
by using bounded
staleness reads.

00:34:15.250 --> 00:34:19.120
So what we're doing is we've
got some code in our system

00:34:19.120 --> 00:34:24.520
that is attempting to determine
whether a bucket exists.

00:34:24.520 --> 00:34:27.699
And so that's-- a bucket
is represented by a row

00:34:27.699 --> 00:34:29.090
in our database.

00:34:29.090 --> 00:34:32.620
So we first start by issuing
a bounded staleness read

00:34:32.620 --> 00:34:34.120
for that bucket.

00:34:34.120 --> 00:34:37.300
Now, almost all
the time, that read

00:34:37.300 --> 00:34:39.070
is going to return success.

00:34:39.070 --> 00:34:41.679
It's going to say,
OK, the bucket exists.

00:34:41.679 --> 00:34:44.440
And at that point, you've just
issued a bounded staleness

00:34:44.440 --> 00:34:46.159
read, and you're done.

00:34:46.159 --> 00:34:49.270
Now, in the unusual
case, it's going to say,

00:34:49.270 --> 00:34:50.620
that bucket isn't there.

00:34:50.620 --> 00:34:54.489
And in that case, we
retry with a strong read

00:34:54.489 --> 00:34:58.090
to make sure to differentiate
between the case

00:34:58.090 --> 00:35:01.210
where it was just a
bounded staleness read that

00:35:01.210 --> 00:35:04.780
caused that bucket to not
be visible versus the bucket

00:35:04.780 --> 00:35:06.910
actually not being present.

00:35:06.910 --> 00:35:11.380
So in an unusual case, we'll
retry with a strong read

00:35:11.380 --> 00:35:14.830
in order to maintain our
strong consistency guarantees,

00:35:14.830 --> 00:35:20.950
but we still get a big
performance optimization.

00:35:20.950 --> 00:35:24.570
So to sum up, it's
been a lot simpler

00:35:24.570 --> 00:35:26.700
for Google Cloud
Storage to build

00:35:26.700 --> 00:35:29.790
our industry-leading
functionality, reliability,

00:35:29.790 --> 00:35:32.490
and scale because
we've gone all in

00:35:32.490 --> 00:35:35.340
on building our system
on top of Spanner.

00:35:35.340 --> 00:35:38.850
And we take advantage of
Spanner's strong consistency,

00:35:38.850 --> 00:35:43.050
its simple programming
model, its high availability,

00:35:43.050 --> 00:35:46.560
and its ability to
scale horizontally.

00:35:46.560 --> 00:35:50.210
[MUSIC PLAYING]

